    "diff_hunk": "@@ -1296,6 +1297,40 @@ these steps:\n <p class=example id=example-string-concatenate>To serialize a set <var>set</var>, return the\n <a for=string>concatenation</a> of <var>set</var> using U+0020 SPACE.\n \n+<h3 id=time>Time</h3>\n+\n+<p>An <dfn export>instant</dfn> is a point in time, independent of any calendar\n+or time zone. The <dfn export>Unix epoch</dfn> is the [=instant=] at 00:00:00\n+UTC on January 1, 1970. <dfn export>Now</dfn> is the [=instant=] when an\n+algorithm executes the step using [=now=]. Two different uses of [=now=] return\n+different [=instants=], so most algorithms should save it into a variable once,\n+and use that variable multiple times.\n+\n+<p>A <dfn export>duration</dfn> is a length of time, the distance from one\n+[=instant=] to another one (or the sum of several such distances). [=Durations=]\n+are signed: the [=duration=] from a later [=instant=] to an earlier one is\n+negative.\n+\n+When exchanging [=instants=] and [=durations=] with Javascript, use\n+{{DOMHighResTimeStamp}}, which represents a [=duration=] as a {{double}} number\n+of milliseconds. To represent an [=instant=] as a {{DOMHighResTimeStamp}}, use\n+the duration from the [=Unix epoch=] to that [=instant=].\n+\n+<div class=example id=example-times>\n+ <p><code>new {{Date}}(<var>value</var>)</code> represents the [=instant=] that\n+ is <var>value</var> milliseconds after the [=Unix epoch=].\n+\n+ <p>{{Performance/now()|performance.now()}} returns the [=duration=]\n+ from the [=environment settings object/time origin=] to [=now=],\n+ represented as a {{double}} number of milliseconds, with its resolution limited\n+ by the [=coarsen time=] algorithm.\n+</div>\n+\n+Time gets more complicated the longer you study it. It's possible for a use of",
